title: Store tables
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: The key-value store's tables, primary and derived, and how their keys are built.

The store is a set of LMDB tables, each an ''ordered map of byte strings'': a read is
a lookup or a scan of a key range. Everything below is designed around that — keys
are built so that what a read needs together is one contiguous range.

!! How keys are built

* Integers are big-endian, so byte order is numeric order.
* A uuid is its 16 bytes.
* A name inside a composite key (a field name, a node's name) is escaped (`00` → `00 FF`) and ended by `00 00`, so that no name is a prefix of another: `tag` and `tags` never share a range.
* `·` below separates the parts of a composite key.

!! Primary tables (`kvstore/mod.rs`)

What was written. The rest of the daemon reaches it through `Rows` and `Log`
([[The storage boundary]]).

|!Table |!Key |!Value |!Serves |
|`meta` |name |the id counters, HEAD, the layout and derived-format versions, the watcher's label | |
|`metarecords` |uuid |empty |existence (the version is computed from the rows, [[Metarecord version]]) |
|`records` |dense id |the rows: field name · value, each; then, if any, their numbers, tombs and cleared fields |a record's rows, in one read |
|`mass` |part · operation number |field · kind · values · the ids of the part |the writes to many records not yet written into the records ([[Mass writes in the store]]) |
|`field_types` |field name · value type |how many rows of that type |a field's type ([[One value type per field name]]) |
|`forest` |field · parent · name bytes |uuid · dense id |a child by name: path lookup |
|`ops` |op id |the operation — or, at its first id, a stub: one entry for the operations of a write to many records ([[Mass operations in the log]]) |the log, in order ([[The operation log]]) |
|`snaps` |op id · after? · index |a snapshot row |what an operation changed, before and after |
|`ops_by_label` |label · op id |— |an undo or revert by label |
|`ops_by_entity` |uuid · op id |— |a metarecord's history; under the nil uuid, the stubs |
|`labels` |label id |name · description · parent · created |[[Labels and contexts]] |
|`label_kids` |parent label · child label |— |the labels' nesting |
|`label_keys` |command key |label id |a command finding its label at its next write |
|`reverted_by` |op id · op id of a revert of it |— |whether an operation still stands ([[The undo selection]]) |
|`watcher_ops`, `user_ops` |op id |— |the two retention classes ([[Automatic retention]]) |

A record's rows are ''one key'', its dense id, in `records`: reading a record is one
read, and so is writing it, whatever its fields — a real file carries a dozen `mfr_*`
and metadata fields. The price is that a read of one field reads the others with it.
A row has no key of its own and no id: it is its name and value in its record's list,
in no order. Operation and label ids come from counters in `meta` and are never reused.

What the rows of ''one field'' of the whole repository are is not read off the rows:
the field's counts and its absences (the bitmaps below) say which records hold one,
and those records are read.

The dense id is a number the derived tables hand out (`ids`, `uuids`), and `records`
and `forest` are filed under it, so a reindex, which numbers the metarecords again,
files them again: the records go under their uuid while the ids are numbered, then
under their new id. A metarecord with no row has no key in `records`.

A deleted metarecord's `records` key goes with it; its `forest` key stays until a
reindex: a `forest` entry carries its metarecord's dense id, tested against the
universe ([[Deleting a metarecord in the store]]).

!! Derived tables — the indexes (`kvstore/derived.rs`)

Computed from the primary tables, read by the query evaluator through `KvSource`.

|!Table |!Key |!Value |
|`ids` |uuid |dense id (u32) |
|`uuids` |dense id |uuid |
|`sets` |kind · [field] · [key] · chunk |a roaring bitmap of dense ids |
|`parts` |field · partition · key · dense id |rows (count) |
|`kids` |parent's dense id · field · dense id |rows (count) |
|`grams` |`0` · field · trigram · text key |empty: a distinct text holding the trigram |
|`grams` |`1` · field · trigram · chunk |a roaring bitmap of dense ids |
|`grams` |`2` · field |the field's trigram state: its mode, its counts |
|`hist` |field · bucket |rows (count) |
|`events` |field · number |a pending event of the field's values: kind · values · a bitmap of dense ids ([[Mass writes in the store]]) |

''Dense ids'' come first: each metarecord gets a small integer, allocated in
discovery order and never reused. Bitmaps hold these ids, not uuids. A deleted
metarecord's id leaves the ''universe'' and stays everywhere else, so every answer
is read through the universe ([[Deleting a metarecord in the store]]). The files of one
folder mostly get consecutive ids, so a folder's bitmaps compress to a few runs.

Moves and late discoveries scatter a subtree's ids over the years; `mf repo reindex`
gathers them again ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]). It numbers the
metarecords in ''forest order'' — each forest walked depth first, a folder's
children by name, the forest of the most positions first — then those in no forest,
in discovery order. A record comes at its first position, so after a reindex every
subtree of the largest forest is one run of ids.

A ''set'' is a bitmap cut in chunks of 65 536 ids (the high 16 bits), one key per
chunk: a write rewrites one chunk, a read unions a few. The kinds:

|!Kind |!Keyed by |!Holds |
|universe |— |every metarecord |
|counts |field · bit |how many values of the field each id holds, one set per bit of the count (below) |
|absent |field |the ids holding `Nothing` in it |
|parents |field |on a `tree_ref` field, the ids with a child |
|referrers |field · target's dense id |the ids pointing at a metarecord — a folder's children, a `ref`'s referrers ([[The forest in the store]]) |
|descendants |field · node's dense id |every id below a node ([[The forest in the store]]) |
|postings |field · value key |the holders of a frequent value ([[Sorting and postings]]) |
|long texts |field |the ids whose text is too long for trigrams ([[Text search in the store]]) |
|pending trigrams |field |in a field whose trigrams are kept by metarecord, the ids written since the last fold ([[Text search in the store]]) |
|reserved |— |the ids given to a uuid something points at and no metarecord has had |

!! The count of values

How many values of a field a record holds is kept as ''one set per bit of the
count'': a record holding five tags (binary 101) is in the sets of bits 0 and 2. Two
questions asked all the time are read off them — a record ''holds a value'' when it
is in any of the sets, and ''holds several'' when it is in any but the first (what a
sorted walk needs, [[Sorting and postings]]) — and so are the schema check's "more
than, fewer than so many values" ([[Schema]]), by comparing the counts with the
bound bit by bit from the highest, without reading a record (`tests/kv_cost.rs`: four
such questions read 192 144 keys on 16 000 records of three tags each, under 300
now). A field of single values has one set, as it had one `present` set before
(November 2026: `present` and `multi` were sets of their own).

Why a count and not two flags: a write to many records at once can keep a count
without reading any of them. Adding a value to the records that lack it is ''+1'' on
a set of ids, which on these bitmaps is a carry — a few set operations whatever the
number of ids — and removing one a borrow; "holds several" cannot be kept that way,
since whether a record that loses a value still holds several depends on how many it
had. A `Nothing` row is not a value: it is the `absent` set, beside the count.

A ''partition'' (`parts`) lists a field's rows in the order of a key whose bytes sort
like the values, so equality, ranges and sorts are range scans. Two per field:
''value'' (the value itself) and ''name'' (a `tree_ref`'s name). A third, by the uuid
a value points at, was dropped in November 2026: a key per reference and per position
written for one reader, which the referrers answer.

!! Filed under the target's dense id

What points at a metarecord — the referrers of a `ref`, the children and descendants
of a folder — is filed under that metarecord's ''dense id'', not its uuid: following a
reference from a set of ids reads their bitmaps without translating each id to a uuid,
and the keys are 12 bytes shorter. A forest's roots are filed under id 0, which no
metarecord has. A uuid pointed at before any metarecord has it (a reference to a
record not yet created, a child written before its parent) is given an id at once,
kept in the ''reserved'' set; the metarecord takes it when it comes.

A dense id is not a metarecord's for life, so what is filed under one follows it:

* a metarecord deleted and created again (a revert) gets a new id ([[Deleting a metarecord in the store]]); its referrers, descendants and `kids` are moved under the new one in the same write — a few keys per field holding references;
* `mf repo reindex` numbers every metarecord again and rebuilds all of this. Whatever else holds a dense id must be rewritten by it — today the `forest` entries — and what a client holds is refused afterwards: a page's cursor is bound to the ''numbering'', a counter the reindex moves ([[Pagination and sorting]]).

`kids` is the forest keyed by parent first, so a metarecord arriving or leaving finds
the fields it is a parent in without a scan. `grams` holds the trigrams of a
field's texts, naming the distinct texts or the ids that hold each ([[Text search in the store]]). `hist` counts a number's or a date's rows per bucket,
a bucket being a fixed-width prefix of the value key: the histograms the planner
estimates a range from ([[Query planner]]).

Every row change goes through one primitive (`derive_row`), every metarecord creation
and removal through two others (`derive_created`, `derive_removed`) — reverts
included —, so the indexes are maintained in each write's own transaction; `mf repo
check` holds all of them to a rebuild, on the ids of the metarecords that exist.

!! Key size

LMDB refuses a key over 511 bytes, and a partition key (holding a value) or a forest
key (holding a name) can hold any text:

* ''A text over 256 escaped bytes'' is keyed by that prefix, a marker `00 01` and a 64-bit hash of the whole. The marker sorts after the `00 00` ending a text equal to the prefix and before anything longer, so order is kept — except among long texts sharing the prefix, which a reader resolves from the values.
* ''A node name over 300 bytes'' is keyed by its first 300 bytes and a hash, and read back from its row.
* ''A field name'' is part of every key: one over about 200 bytes cannot be written.
